<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Firth of Forth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Firth of Forth is the estuary of the River Forth on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> on its southern shore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> to the north. In Roman and early medieval times it marked a frontier zone between the Votadini and Gododdin to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> to the north. Northumbrian power reached it in the 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth.jpg" alt="Firth of Forth" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Nugent · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Forth_bridges_from_the_air_(geograph_5835049).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Firth of Forth is a bay and inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> that separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> to the north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> to the south. Further inland it becomes the estuary of the River Forth and several other rivers. It was formed as a fjord by the Forth Glacier in the last glacial period, which scoured out the existing Forth Valley, and over time natural processes turned most of it into a wide bay with gently sloping banks. Its name combines the Scots word firth, a cognate of fjord, with Forth, from a Proto-Celtic word meaning slow running. It was Bodotria in Roman times, Myrkvifiörd in the Norse sagas and, in an early Welsh name, the sea of Iudeu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> reportedly made a bridge of around 900 boats, probably at South Queensferry. In the 10th century the river was regarded as the boundary of Scotia and was sometimes called the Scots Water. Culross, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> shore, was established in the 6th century and was the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kentigern">Saint Kentigern</a>. The inner firth, between the Kincardine and Forth bridges, has lost about half of its former intertidal area to land reclamation, partly for farming but mainly for industry.</p><p>The firth was the site of heavy industry and docks, among them Grangemouth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a>, Methil, Inverkeithing and the naval dockyard at Rosyth. It is crossed by bridges at two places. The Forth Bridge, a railway bridge on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> line, was begun in 1882 and opened on 4 March 1890. The Kincardine Bridge opened in October 1936 and the Forth Road Bridge on 4 September 1964. From 1964 to 1982 a tunnel dug by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> miners ran under the firth, linking the Kinneil colliery on the south side with the Valleyfield colliery on the north. The Clackmannanshire Bridge was completed in November 2008, and the Queensferry Crossing, begun in 2011, was formally opened on 4 September 2017.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The firth is a Site of Special Scientific Interest. The Firth of Forth Islands Special Protection Area holds more than 90,000 breeding seabirds every year, and there is a bird observatory on the Isle of May. Its islands include Bass Rock, Inchcolm, Inchkeith and the Isle of May. The European oyster, fished to extinction here by the 1900s, has been reintroduced in the 21st century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a> and Kirkcaldy are among the many towns on its shores.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Firth of Forth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Áedán mac Gabráin, who according to Adomnán was ordained king by Columba, campaigned widely: the annals record his expedition to Orkney around 580 and a battle around 582 in Manann, either the Isle of Man or Manaw on the <b>Forth</b>, and Adomnán describes his battle against the Miathi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Historia Brittonum, held that Cunedda came with his sons from Manaw Gododdin, the district around the head of the <b>Firth of Forth</b>, and expelled Irish settlers from Wales; many historians doubt the story or its date, but the later kings of Gwynedd all traced their line to him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From then Northumbria stretched from the Humber to the <b>Firth of Forth</b>, absorbing the British kingdoms of Elmet, Rheged and Gododdin and reaching west to the Irish Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Scotland grew out of the union of the Picts, who held most of the land north of the <b>Forth</b>, and the Gaelic kingdom of Dál Riata in Argyll.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Picts were the peoples of Scotland north of the <b>Forth</b> and Clyde in late Roman and early medieval times.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hadrian's Wall, begun around 122 and running some 73 miles from the Tyne to the Solway, became the main frontier; the Antonine Wall between the <b>Forth</b> and the Clyde, built from about 142, was held for only some twenty years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li></ul></section>
